{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9fbb03048a02d9f0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tscore: 9\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n9\t  description: 'E004 and E006 both author the TrustyAI guardrails integration documentation\n10\t    with identical content scope (configuration, capabilities, and limitations): E004\n11\t    has scope item Author integration documentation covering supported configuration,\n12\t    capabilities, and limitations plus AC Integration documentation is published,\n13\t    vs E006 scope item Documentation of the supported TrustyAI guardrails integration\n14\t    (configuration, capabilities, and limitations). Two epics owned by different teams\n15\t    (TrustyAI vs RHOAI GenAI) independently produce the same documentation deliverable.\n16\t    Assign the integration documentation to exactly one epic and make the other explicitly\n17\t    consume/uplift it.'\n18\t- severity: minor\n19\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n20\t  description: E003 publishes the SLO targets, backport policy, and supported configuration\n21\t    matrix while E006 also documents the same three posture artifacts; the authoritative-artifact\n22\t    vs doc-set-integration ownership is not delineated. State which epic owns each\n23\t    publishing surface.\n24\t- severity: major\n25\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n26\t  description: E002 scope includes adding a rollback/compatibility safeguard for API\n27\t    changes where the strategy replaces TP-era behavior, but no acceptance criterion\n28\t    verifies it - the rule-mandated rollback AC for a replacement epic is missing.\n29\t    Add a testable AC for the rollback/compatibility safeguard.\n30\t- severity: minor\n31\t  criterion: Completeness\n32\t  description: 'The error-handling dimension of HLR-1 assessment has no explicit downstream\n33\t    home: E002 closes API-stability and upgrade-path gaps and E003 scope incorporates\n34\t    only scale-limit findings, so fix-for-GA error-handling gaps are claimed by no\n35\t    epic. Add explicit error-handling coverage to E002 or E003 scope (or record it\n36\t    in the gate_failure_impact rewrite path).'\n37\terror: null\n38\t---\n39\t# Decomposition Review: RHAISTRAT-9014\n40\t\n41\t## Review Summary\n42\t\n43\tScore: 9/14 — fail\n44\tRecommendation: revise\n45\t\n46\tcontract validator: OK (all mechanical checks passed: epic body sections,\n47\tcanonical components, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance,\n48\timplementation-type parallelism). Triage check: `triage` is null (full\n49\tdecomposition), so the 6-epic shape is expected and not penalized. The\n50\tfailures below are semantic issues the validator cannot check.\n51\t\n52\t## Criterion Details\n53\t\n54\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n55\t\n56\tAll six HLRs map to exactly one epic each (HLR-1→E001, HLR-2→E002,\n57\tHLR-3→E003, HLR-4→E004, HLR-5→E005, HLR-6→E006); the traceability matrix is\n58\tpresent in the summary and every epic carries an HLR Traceability section.\n59\tP2 HLR-6 is covered (not deferred) by E006. Priority inheritance is clean:\n60\tE001 (P0) gates only P0 work (E002/E003). E006 (P0) depends on P1 epics\n61\tE004/E005, but E006 is `docs-authoring` and exempt from priority\n62\tinheritance — its P0 derives correctly from the strategy's Critical\n63\tpriority via the documented exception, and the summary records this\n64\texplicitly. No priority collapse: each epic maps to a single HLR; nothing\n65\tis bundled.\n66\t\n67\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n68\t\n69\tDiagram and frontmatter match exactly in both directions: E002/E003 depend\n70\ton E001; E006 depends on E002/E003/E004/E005; E001/E004/E005 have no\n71\tdependencies. Arrows follow the dependency→dependent convention. No\n72\tcycles, no invalid references. Rule 11 is satisfied: the docs-authoring\n73\tepic (E006) is blocked by