ails integration\n102\t(configuration, capabilities, and limitations)\". These are the same\n103\tdeliverable, word-for-word in content scope, owned by two different teams\n104\t(TrustyAI vs RHOAI GenAI) — the boundary was drawn incorrectly. The\n105\tcorrect shape is one owning epic for the integration documentation with\n106\tthe other explicitly consuming/uplifting it. A related lesser overlap:\n107\tE003 \"publishes\" the SLO targets, backport policy, and supported\n108\tconfiguration matrix while E006 also documents the same three artifacts;\n109\tthe authoritative-artifact vs doc-set-integration split is not delineated.\n110\t\n111\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n112\t\n113\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (the definitive GA scope)\n114\tchanges what E002/E003 contain, per the strategy's own open question, and\n115\tboth dependents exist. E002-E006 are Implementations producing concrete\n116\tartifacts (versioned APIs and policies, posture documents, a default-flag\n117\tchange, a documentation set). Gating metadata is fully correct: E002/E003\n118\teach have `gated_by: E001`, E001 appears in their direct `dependencies`,\n119\tand `gate_failure_impact` carries both `action: rewrite` and a substantive\n120\tfallback approach. No epic lists an Investigation dependency without\n121\t`gated_by`; no `gated_by` target is only transitively reachable.\n122\t\n123\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n124\t\n125\tSignal fields are type-correct: E001 carries `investigation_signals`\n126\t(5-signal rubric); E002-E006 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric); no\n127\tcrossover. Rationales in the ai-signals files match the frontmatter values\n128\tand are well-grounded. Spot checks hold up: E005's high score is\n129\tconsistent with an exact, precedent-backed flag flip; E002/E003's\n130\t`open_questions: -1` matches their `gated_by` status; E004's\n131\t`open_questions: 0` is consistent with the DAG decision that its approach\n132\tis fixed by HLR-4. The `architecture_claims: 1` scores cite\n133\t`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/llama-stack.md`,\n134\t`trustyai.md`, and `dashboard.md` — all three files exist and corroborate\n135\tthe Tech Preview premise, so the calibration is sound, not a\n136\t\"no architecture claims\" miss.\n137\t\n138\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (0/2)\n139\t\n140\tEach epic has four concrete, mostly testable ACs; E005 has a proper\n141\trollback AC (verified disable path); E006 has the rule-mandated technical\n142\treview AC; no konflux chain exists so no build-pipeline AC applies; no\n143\tdraft-ID references appear in epic bodies; no externally-controlled\n144\tsoft-ACs found (stakeholder agreement ACs in E001/E003 condition on the\n145\tepic's own deliverable). However, E002 is missing its rule-mandated\n146\trollback AC: the scope explicitly includes \"Add a rollback/compatibility\n147\tsafeguard for API changes where the strategy replaces TP-era behavior\",\n148\tbut no acceptance criterion verifies that safeguard — the four ACs cover\n149\tversioning, upgrade execution, gap closure, and deprecation-policy\n150\tprocess, none test rollback/compatibility. A replacement epic's rollback\n151\tsafeguard sitting in scope with no verifying AC is a missing\n152\trule-mandated AC.\n153\t\n154\t### 7. Completeness (1/2)\n155\t\n156\tCore scope is covered: all six HLRs have epics, the out-of-scope bounds\n157\tare respected (E004 explicitly excludes new guardrails feature\n158\tdevelopment), Open Question 1 is carried forward as E001's gating purpose,\n159\tand the strategy's missing-sections gap (no teams, no ACs, no NFRs) is\n160\trecorded in Health Warnings with owners sourced from architecture\n161\tcontext. One minor scope gap: HLR-1's assessment inventories gaps across\n162\tfour dimensions, but only three have an explicit downstream home — E002\n163\tcloses \"API-stability and upgrade-path gaps... dispositioned as\n164\tfix-for-GA\" and E003's matrix incorporates \"t