{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bbe0249707529006","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition: RHAISTRAT-9014 — Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done, priority Critical. Llama Stack shipped as Tech\n13\tPreview; GA requires closing quality gaps whose full inventory is unknown. The\n14\tstrategy's single open question states that the GA scope itself depends on the\n15\tHLR-1 assessment — this makes the assessment a true gate for the hardening work.\n16\t\n17\t## Epic List\n18\t\n19\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n20\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | GA-readiness assessment: Llama Stack TP-to-GA gap inventory and definitive GA scope | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantees and tested upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | TrustyAI guardrails as a supported, documented GA integration | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Dashboard Llama Stack management enabled by default | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift and TP migration notes | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n27\t\n28\tNote on E006 priority: derived as P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception\n29\t(strategy frontmatter priority: Critical), even though HLR-6 is P2. See Health\n30\tWarnings.\n31\t\n32\tNote on documentation ownership (publishing surfaces): the TrustyAI guardrails\n33\tintegration documentation is authored by E004 (the TrustyAI supported-integration\n34\tepic) as its owning deliverable, per HLR-4's \"supported, documented integration\".\n35\tThe three support-posture artifacts (SLO targets, backport policy, supported\n36\tconfiguration matrix) are authored and published as the authoritative artifacts\n37\tby E003. E006 (docs-authoring) integrates and uplifts these owned deliverables\n38\tinto the GA documentation set — it does not re-author them. Downstream homes for\n39\tthe assessment's gap dimensions: API-stability, upgrade-path, and error-handling\n40\tgaps dispositioned fix-for-GA are closed by E002; scale-limit (and\n41\terror-handling) findings are incorporated into E003's supported configuration\n42\tmatrix.\n43\t\n44\t## Dependency DAG\n45\t\n46\t```mermaid\n47\tgraph TD\n48\t  E001 --> E002\n49\t  E001 --> E003\n50\t  E002 --> E006\n51\t  E003 --> E006\n52\t  E004 --> E006\n53\t  E005 --> E006\n54\t```\n55\t\n56\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E006 (length 3).\n57\t\n58\t## DAG Justification\n59\t\n60\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n61\t|------|------|-----------|\n62\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream Implementation) | Open Question 1: until the GA-readiness assessment lands, the size of the stability and hardening work is an estimate. The assessment produces the definitive GA scope, so the API-stability/hardening epic is a true gate (`rewrite`: scope/approach changes with the findings, including which API-stability, upgrade-path, and error-handling gaps are dispositioned fix-for-GA). |\n63\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream Implementation) | The supported configuration matrix must incorporate the scale limits (and error-handling findings) the assessment inventories; the support-posture epic's content changes with the outcome (`rewrite`). |\n64\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Migration notes and GA docs must describe the shipped API stability guarantees and upgrade path. |\n65\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 | The GA documentation set integrates the authoritative support-posture artifacts (SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix) published by the support-posture epic. |\n66\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 | The GA documentation set integrates and uplifts the TrustyAI guardrails integration documentation authored by the TrustyAI supported-integration epic (the owning deliverable), rather than re-authoring it. |\n67\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 | Docs must reflect default Dashboard enablement and remove TP opt-in instructions. |\n68\t| (no edge) E001 → E004 | Rule 4 / Step 3 decision rule | The TrustyAI integration promotion is fully specified by HLR-4 (experimental → supported + documented); the assessment's enumerated gap dimensions (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling) do not change which epics exist or what this epic does. GA-bar shortfalls found here are fix-and-retry acceptance criteria, not a gate. |\n69\t| (no edge) E001 → E005 | Rule 4 / Step 3 decision rule | Default enablement for entitled clusters is a concrete, self-contained change unaffected by the assessment outcome. |\n70\t| (no edge) E004 ↔ E005 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifacts) | TrustyAI operator work and Dashboard default-flag work share no artifacts; parallel. |\n71\t\n72\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n73\t\n74\t| HLR | Priority | Epic |\n75\t|-----|----------|------|\n76\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 |\n77\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 |\n78\t| HLR-3 | P0 | E003 |\n79\t| HLR-4 | P1 | E004 |\n80\t| HLR-5 | P1 | E005 |\n81\t| HLR-6 | P2 | E006 |\n82\t\n83\tAll six HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements; no bundled rows (each HLR maps\n84\tto exactly one epic).\n85\t\n86\t## Health Warnings\n87\t\n88\t- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** the strategy provides no\n89\t  Impacted Teams table, no per-component change/owner detail (only a component\n90\t  list), no Dependencies table, no formal Given/When/Then acceptance criteria,\n91\t  and no NFRs. Owner teams were taken from architecture context\n92\t  (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`: Llama Stack →\n93\t  RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI → TrustyAI, Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard). The\n94\t  documentation epic's team (RHOAI GenAI) is an assumption — the strategy names\n95\t  no docs team; it is assigned to the Llama Stack owning team as the component\n96\t  being documented.\n97\t- **Potential priority inversion (non-blocking, per rule):** E006 carries P0\n98\t  (docs-authoring exception: strategy priority Critical → P0) while its source\n99\t  HLR-6 is P2. This is rule-mandated, but a human planner may want to treat the\n100\t  docs work as schedulable at P2 urgency.\n101\t- **Sizing is an estimate until E001 lands (Open Question 1):** E002 and E003\n102\t  are scoped from HLR text now; their true size follows the assessment. Both\n103\t  carry `gated_by` E001 with `rewrite` gate-failure impact. If the assessment\n104\t  surfaces hardening work far beyond the HLR-2/HLR-3 text, re-split E002 along\n105\t  the assessment's gap categories (API stability vs. upgrade path vs.\n106\t  error handling) rather than growing it unboundedly.\n107\t- **E004 spans two components:** HLR-4's integration work touches both TrustyAI\n108\t  and the Llama Stack distribution surface. Per the single-most-relevant-\n109\t  component rule it is assigned canonical component `TrustyAI` (the guardrails\n110\t  are \"via TrustyAI\"); cross-team coordination with RHOAI GenAI is recorded as\n111\t  a risk on the epic. If Llama Stack-distribution-side wiring turns out to be\n112\t  substantial code work, split it into a RHOAI GenAI-owned implementation epic\n113\t  blocked by this one (Rule 12).\n114\t- **Architecture claims corroborated:** all three components have architecture\n115\t  context files (`rhoai-3.0/llama-stack.md`, `trustyai.md`, `dashboard.md`)\n116\t  corroborating the strategy's Tech Preview premise for Llama Stack — no\n117\t  contradictions found. Llama Stack is Konflux-onboarded and midstream-hosted,\n118\t  so no onboarding chain epics are needed.\n119\t\n120\t## Tiered Delivery\n121\t\n122\tNot applicable in the Rule 16-18 sense — there is no external dependency with\n123\tuncertain timing (no upstream PR/RFC or vendor gating). The internal gating\n124\tunknown (E001) blocks only E002/E003; E004 (TrustyAI integration) and E005\n125\t(Dashboard default enablement) deliver independent partial value and run in\n126\tparallel from the start, which is the practical Tier 1 / gated split for this\n127\tstrategy.\n128\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a239b36a113a9dd1","agent_id":"a3ecef42eb32128d9","session_id":"45850d2e-0f91-4d60-8339-ec068a16e17f","uuid":"d0b71d87-4b72-4a66-86db-648f9f863a26","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:30:42.519Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bbe0249707529006","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
