ks/Notes — a defensible boundary, not a violation. Scope duplication is\nexplicitly designed out: E003 authors the three support-posture artifacts and\nE004 authors the integration documentation as owning deliverables, while E006\nintegrates without re-authoring, enforced by E006's \"exactly once, no\nduplicated or divergent re-authoring\" acceptance criterion — the right shape.\nSize: E002 is the largest epic and its estimate-above-guideline risk is\nrecorded in Health Warnings with estimate, why-not-split rationale (scoped from\nHLR text now; true size follows the assessment), and a concrete conditional\nre-split trigger (re-split along the assessment's gap categories rather than\ngrowing unboundedly) — a watch item, not a split demand. E004's inclusion of\nthe integration documentation as an owned deliverable was considered under\nwork-product type mixing and accepted: HLR-4 defines \"supported, documented\nintegration\" as one requirement, and assigning content ownership to the\nimplementing epic while E006 (docs-authoring) performs the GA-standards uplift\navoids the duplication the alternative shape would create.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nE001 is a genuine Investigation: the strategy's Open Question 1 states the GA\nscope itself is unknown until the assessment lands, and two downstream epics\n(E002, E003) change scope/approach on its outcome — the investigation's result\nchanges what downstream epics do. All Implementation epics produce concrete\nartifacts (versioned API surface + policies, posture artifacts, supported\nintegration, default flip, docs set). Gating metadata is complete and correct:\nE002 and E003 both set `gated_by: E001` with `gate_failure_impact` populated\nwith both `action: rewrite` and a concrete `fallback_approach`, and the\n`gated_by` target appears in each epic's direct `dependencies` list. E004, E005,\nand E006 have no Investigation dependencies and correctly have `gated_by: null`\n— E004's rationale (approach fixed by HLR-4;